.\"
.\" ULIMIT(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH ULIMIT 2 "2026-08-22" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
ulimit \- get and set process limits (legacy System V interface)
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RB ( libc ", " \-l:libc.so.0 )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ulimit.h>
.PP
.BI "long ulimit(int " cmd ", long " newlimit ");
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR ulimit ()
is the System V predecessor of
.BR getrlimit (2)
and
.BR setrlimit (2).
It operates on the very same limits: a change made here is visible through
.BR getrlimit (2)
and vice versa.
New code should use
.BR setrlimit (2),
which can express every resource rather than these four commands.
.PP
.I cmd
is one of:
.TP
.B UL_GETFSIZE
Return the file-size limit
.RB ( RLIMIT_FSIZE )
in units of 512-byte blocks.
.TP
.B UL_SETFSIZE
Set the file-size limit to
.I newlimit
512-byte blocks.
.TP
.B UL_GETMAXBRK
Return the highest value
.BR brk (2)
could reach, derived from
.BR RLIMIT_DATA .
.TP
.B UL_GETMAXFDS
Return the descriptor limit
.RB ( RLIMIT_NOFILE ).
.PP
The file-size commands count
.B blocks,
not bytes, which is the classic pitfall of this interface:
.B UL_SETFSIZE
with 2 sets a one-kilobyte limit.
.PP
As with
.BR setrlimit (2),
only a process with an effective UID of 0 may raise a limit above its current
hard ceiling.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success the value of the requested limit is returned.
On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.PP
Because
.B RLIM_INFINITY
cannot be represented in a
.IR long ,
an unlimited file size is reported as
.BR LONG_MAX .
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I cmd
is not one of the commands above, or
.I newlimit
is negative.
.TP
.B EPERM
An unprivileged process tried to raise the file-size limit above its hard
ceiling.
.SH EXAMPLE
Cap created files at 1 MiB using the legacy interface:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
/* 2048 blocks * 512 bytes = 1 MiB */
if (ulimit(UL_SETFSIZE, 2048) == \-1) {
    perror("ulimit");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}
.EE
.in
.SH NOTES
This call is also reached by the SCO Xenix/286 personality, where it is
system call 63, so Xenix-era binaries that predate
.BR setrlimit (2)
get the same limits as native ones.
.PP
Do not confuse this with the shell's
.B ulimit
builtin, which is a front end to
.BR setrlimit (2).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR getrlimit (2),
.BR brk (2),
.BR open (2)
